= Law of diminishing returns — Simple =

<onlyinclude>The '''law of diminishing returns''' in economics describes a situation in which adding more of one productive input, while holding other relevant inputs and technology fixed, eventually adds smaller and smaller amounts of extra output. It is a statement about '''marginal output''', not a claim that total production must fall or that every activity always becomes less useful with practice.<ref name="openstax">OpenStax, [https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/7-2-production-in-the-short-run ''Principles of Economics'', §7.2, Production in the Short Run], marginal product and fixed inputs.</ref>

== An example ==

A small bakery has one oven. Hiring another baker can increase the number of loaves produced per hour, but if many bakers share that single oven, each additional baker may add less output than the previous one.

At some point the oven, not the available labor, becomes the main limitation. Buying another oven could change the situation; that is why the fixed-input assumption matters.

== Total output versus marginal output ==

Suppose an illustrative workshop produces the following numbers of units per day:

{| class="wikitable"
! Workers !! Total output !! Extra output from one more worker
|-
| 1 || 10 || —
|-
| 2 || 24 || 14
|-
| 3 || 35 || 11
|-
| 4 || 43 || 8
|-
| 5 || 48 || 5
|}

Here total production continues to rise from three to five workers, but each added worker contributes less than the previous one. This is diminishing marginal product, not negative output growth.

The numbers are hypothetical, not measurements of a real workplace.

== Does the law always apply? ==

No. New tools, improved teamwork, learning or changes in technology can raise productivity. If capital, space or coordination improve together with labor, the production conditions differ. Economists usually discuss diminishing marginal returns with other inputs held constant over a relevant range.

Sometimes a small team gains efficiency as it grows before eventually encountering bottlenecks. The phrase “eventually diminishing” recognizes that increasing marginal product may occur at low input levels.

== Is this the same as diminishing utility? ==

No. '''Diminishing marginal utility''' refers to changes in additional satisfaction or value from consuming more of something, within a chosen preference framework. The law of diminishing marginal product concerns physical or economic production output as an input is increased.

Both use the idea of a marginal change, but they describe different things and require different evidence.

== Does it contradict scaling? ==

Not necessarily. A firm might obtain less output from each additional worker while its number of ovens remains fixed, yet double its entire operation successfully by doubling both workers and ovens. This is a difference between changing one input and changing all inputs.

Scaling and optimization study related questions about changing system size and allocating limited resources.

== Where is it useful? ==

The concept helps explain why bottlenecks form in agriculture, production, service teams and some computational systems. But a real claim requires measurements of marginal productivity and the inputs that remain unchanged.

A company should not blame employees for diminishing returns when the observed limitation is inadequate equipment or an inappropriate performance measure.

